meta { title = "CPU parallelism", description = "Spawn typed tasks and keep their lifetimes structured.", order = 1, group = "HOWTO guides" }

Ordinary function calls run synchronously in the current execution context. spawn creates a typed CPU task; await observes its completed result. Place task work inside a parallel scope, then handle or propagate the TaskError channel.

h2 { "Start independent work before awaiting" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn double(value: int) -> int:
  value * 2

fn main() -> int ! TaskError:
  try parallel:
    left = spawn double(10)
    right = spawn double(11)
    a = try await left
    b = try await right
    a + b
""" }

Both tasks are created before either is awaited, so the scheduler can run them concurrently. spawn followed immediately by await is useful for placement or boundaries, but a sequence of such pairs does not expose the same independent overlap.

h2 { "A parallel scope owns its children" }

Tasks use captured immutable values and return typed completed values. A scope settles its children before exiting. Awaiting suspends the coordinating continuation rather than occupying a worker thread while waiting. Repeated awaits observe the cached result; they do not rerun the task.

h2 { "Map an array in parallel" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn main() -> [int] ! TaskError:
  factor = 2
  try parallelMap([1, 2, 3], fn(value: int): value * factor)
""" }

parallelMap returns results in input order and can change the element type under the supported callback rules. map remains sequential. Scheduling a map call with spawn creates a task containing one sequential mapping loop; it does not turn each element into an independent task.

h2 { "Workers and resource limits" }

Generated CPU programs use a bounded worker pool. The detected default is capped at eight; KALANDRA_WORKERS accepts 1 through 64. Task records, ready queues, captures and nested groups have admission limits. Tiny callbacks may cost more to schedule than to execute, so measure your actual workload.

codeblock { language = bash, """KALANDRA_WORKERS=4 ./program
""" }

Cancellation is cooperative, and generated numerical loops poll for it. Worker capacity and admitted work are bounded; a parallel scope does not guarantee which racing failure is observed. GPU placement is explicit and has its own supported numerical subset.

